{
  "dom_id": "step-q344938",
  "name": "Question 6",
  "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14195167/assets/60e04faa7448d351706ee1af70a8620590ca16e0debce7dd2e58631462594132.png)\n\nConsider all circular sectors $DEF$ with area $A=144$ square units. Determine the smallest possible perimeter $P$ of such a circular sector. Do not round the answer.\n\n$P=$\n{{field-1}}\nunits",
  "worked_solution": "We let $r$ be the radius of the circular sector and let $\\theta $ be the angle (in radians) subtended by it. Then, we want to minimize the perimeter\n\n$$\nP=2r+r\\theta .\n$$\n\nWe know that the area is fixed at $144$ square units, so we also have the constraint condition\n\n$$\n144={r}^{2}\\frac{\\theta }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, by making $\\theta $ the subject of the constraint equation and taking into account that $r>0,$ we find that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}144 & =\\frac{1}{2}{r}^{2}\\theta  \\\\ \\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nPlugging this into our expression for $P,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2r+r\\cdot \\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =2r+\\frac{288}{r}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to minimize $P,$ so we need to differentiate $P$ and set the derivative equal to zero. Computing the derivative gives \n\n$$\n{P}^{′}(r)=2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nSetting the derivative equal to zero and solving for $r$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2-\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} & =0 \\\\ 2{r}^{2} & =288 \\\\ {r}^{2} & =144 \\\\ r & =12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we confirm that $r=12$ is a minimum using the second derivative test.\n\nSince the second derivative ${P}^{″}(r)=\\frac{576}{{r}^{3}}>0$, the stationary point $r=12$ is a minimum.\n\nFinally, when $r=12$, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\theta  & =\\frac{288}{{r}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{288}{144} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe dimensions of the circular sector with the smallest perimeter are $r=12$ and $\\theta =2.$ Therefore, the smallest perimeter is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P & =2r+r\\theta  \\\\  & =2\\cdot 12+12\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =24+24 \\\\  & =48.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
